# MySQL — Hostinger hPanel > Databases (free, included in the plan).
# For local dev: docker compose up -d  →  mysql://propia:propia@localhost:3306/propia
#
# This is the app's connection and the one an agent is given. In production it
# should be a **read-only** user (SELECT on the schema): that is enough for
# `npm run db:status` and for every `--dry` run of a `cron:*` / `seed:*` job,
# which is all an automated contributor ever needs. `tsx` does not read this
# file — export the value in the shell before running any script.
DATABASE_URL=mysql://user:password@localhost:3306/propia

# The owner (read-write) user, for the runs that actually write —
# `npm run cron:cuotas` without --dry, `seed:*`, `import:csv`, `user:create`,
# `db:migrate`, and `db:status -- --probe`.
#
# **Keep this on your own machine only and never give it to an agent.** The
# split is the whole safety story of the operations layer: an agent holding the
# read-only DATABASE_URL can look at production and preview any job, and cannot
# change a row. A writing CLI run picks DATABASE_URL_RW ?? DATABASE_URL
# (scripts/db-credential.ts), so leaving this empty is fine on a machine that
# has one credential for both jobs — nothing changes from how it worked before.
# The Next.js app itself always uses DATABASE_URL: on Hostinger that env var is
# the read-write user, because the site inserts leads and listings.
DATABASE_URL_RW=

# Outbound messaging — OPTIONAL. Leads are stored in MySQL first and the panel
# reads them from there, so the portal works with none of these set. A webhook
# adds outbound delivery: a WhatsApp OTP at publish, and a copy of each lead
# pushed to whatever CRM you point it at (GoHighLevel, n8n, your own endpoint).
# Without it the publish wizard skips phone verification instead of issuing a
# code nobody can receive.
#
# The same endpoint also receives operator alerts — {"event":"operator_alert"}
# with kind "new_lead" or "review_submitted" — so a flow can forward "go look"
# to WhatsApp while routing {"event":"lead"} into the CRM. With no webhook set
# there is no alert: /admin badges the review queue and the last 24 h of leads,
# which needs no configuration at all.
LEAD_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Historical alias for LEAD_WEBHOOK_URL; either name works.
GHL_WEBHOOK_URL=
GHL_API_KEY=

# Cloudflare R2 — listing photos. Free tier: 10 GB, zero egress.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=propia-images
# Public delivery URL for the bucket. Use the bucket's own public URL
# (https://pub-<hash>.r2.dev) or a custom domain YOU HAVE MAPPED in
# Cloudflare. Do NOT point this at a domain that isn't set up —
# img.propia.com.py was the old placeholder and propia.com.py is not owned,
# so leaving it would 404 every photo the day R2 goes live (audit F64).
# Leave empty until the bucket exists: empty = stored keys pass through
# unchanged, which keeps imported remote-URL photos working.
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Portal contact identity (src/config/contact.ts) — footer, /contacto,
# privacy policy and Organization JSON-LD all read these. Both are OPTIONAL
# and have no fallback: leave them empty and the site shows no address and no
# WhatsApp CTA, routing everyone to the on-site lead form instead. Fill them
# in only with a mailbox/number that is actually monitored (audit F10).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means Next inlines them at BUILD time — a rebuild is required
# after changing either, not just a restart. Both are per-deployment, not
# per-vertical: the rental doors (alquiler.com.py, rentparaguay.com) read the
# same NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_WHATSAPP as the marketplace doors — there is no
# separate rental WhatsApp number in config (fable/plan-rentparaguay.md §7).
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_WHATSAPP=

# Claude API — barrio guides + EN translations (batch jobs, NEVER the request
# path). `npm run cron:translate` fills listings.title_en/description_en for
# the English door; with no key it refuses to run rather than writing anything,
# and the site is unaffected — publishing never calls the API, so it cannot be
# blocked by one being down (src/lib/translate.ts explains why there is no
# publish-time hook).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional: the model cron:translate uses. Defaults to claude-opus-5.
ANTHROPIC_TRANSLATION_MODEL=

# Translation providers for `cron:translate`, tried cheapest-first: DeepL,
# then Gemini, then Claude (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY above) — any subset may be set;
# a row that fails on one falls through to the next configured provider.
#
# DeepL: a "Developer" tier key ends in ":fx" and carries a ONE-TIME
# 1,000,000-character credit, not a recurring monthly one — run
# cron:translate with --limit and watch usage in the DeepL dashboard rather
# than wide open. Get one at https://www.deepl.com/en/pro-api (Developer tier).
DEEPL_API_KEY=
# Gemini: the cheap ongoing path once DeepL's one-time credit is spent —
# $0.30/$2.50 per million input/output tokens on gemini-3.5-flash-lite (the
# default; override with GEMINI_TRANSLATION_MODEL) with no hidden token
# overhead, unlike gemini-3.6-flash — see src/lib/translate.ts's
# GEMINI_MODEL comment. Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_TRANSLATION_MODEL=

# Canonical host — the Spanish marketplace primary since the D6 flip
# (2026-09-04). It is not only an origin string: src/config/verticals.ts derives
# DEFAULT from it, so it decides the locale, filters and copy of every request
# that arrives without a known Host header. Never edit this and the vertical
# entries separately — they must agree, or an unknown-host request gets one
# door's locale while the known hosts get another's.
#
# Only a host that is a key in src/config/verticals.ts may go here. Several
# domains this project used earlier are not owned by the founder, and pointing
# canonicals at one of those breaks them sitewide.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means Next inlines it at BUILD time: changing it on Hostinger
# needs a rebuild, not a restart, which is why this one item cannot ship in a
# commit (CLAUDE.md backlog 2).
NEXT_PUBLIC_CANONICAL_HOST=inmobiliaria.com.py
